    <DataNode elementId="L-Glutaminyl-Peptides" textLabel="an N-terminal L-glutaminyl-[protein]" type="Metabolite">
      <Xref identifier="MNXM164550" dataSource="MetaNetX" />
      <Graphics centerX="350" centerY="400" width="90.0" height="25.0" textColor="3955e7" fontName="Arial" fontWeight="Normal" fontStyle="Normal" fontDecoration="Normal" fontStrikethru="Normal" fontSize="12" hAlign="Center" vAlign="Middle" borderColor="3955e7" borderStyle="Solid" borderWidth="1.0" fillColor="FFFFFF" shapeType="Rectangle" />
      <Comment source="PMN, zhang, SRI, caspi">In eukaryotes, |FRAME: N-terminal-asparagine &quot;N-terminal Asn&quot;| (N) and |FRAME: L-Glutaminyl-Peptides &quot;N-terminal Gln&quot;| (Q) are tertiary destabilizing residues in that they function, through enzymatic deamidation, to yield the secondary destabilizing N-terminal residues Asp (D) and Glu (E). Asp and Glu are conjugaed by ATE1-encoded Arg-tRNA-protein transferase, to arginine, one of the primary destabilizing N-terminal residues. Arginine is recognized by the E3 Ub ligases of the N-end rule pathway, called N-recognins. A third destabilizing residue that functions through arginylation is an |FRAME: N-terminal-L-cysteine &quot;N-terminal Cys&quot;| (C), which is arginylated after its nitric oxide (NO)-dependent oxidation to |FRAME: N-terminal-L-cysteine-sulfinate| or |FRAME: N-terminal-L-cysteine-sulfonate| (via |FRAME: Protein-nitrosothiol-L-alanine|) |CITS: [16222293]|.</Comment>
